    "Diagnostic-first gate: train_val_gap=-0.0102 (val BETTER than train) -> NO overfit (no_overfit=true), so do NOT prune for regularization (playbook rule 1 \u2014 FS would be neutral-to-harmful on a non-overfit model). iter_0 (eta=0.2,d4,g0.5,cs0.5,143 feats) is already excellent: eval R-p@1=0.895, R-p@3=0.743, R-p@5=0.690, R-p@10=0.572 \u2014 already well above the CatBoost sweep cell (test R-p@3=0.569). anti_auc_flag=unknown, degenerate_sink_warning=false, val_brier 0.0695 > weighted_base_rate 0.061 (not degenerate). Knob family 1 (learning rate): the combine grid showed eta0.05_d4_g0.5 (p1=0.865,p3=0.762,p5=0.701, val_brier 0.0645) lifts the bulk-rank tail (p3/p5) and improves calibration vs the eta=0.2 winner, at a small top-1 cost. I test the midpoint eta=0.1 first as the single cleanest move from the current config \u2014 if it lifts p3/p5/p10 without much R-p@1 loss I'll continue down; if it hurts top-1, I revert and pivot to a different knob family (colsample/depth) per rule 9. Holding everything else fixed (one attributable change per iter, rule 6).",
    "iter_1 (eta=0.1) result confirms playbook rule 9: val_brier IMPROVED (0.0695->0.0657) but eval R-p got WORSE across the board (R-p@1 0.895->0.735, p3 0.743->0.665, p5 0.690->0.627, p10 0.572->0.518). Val movement does NOT track the operating metric here. So eta=0.2 (iter_0) is the learning-rate sweet spot for this d4/g0.5/cs0.5 config; reverting eta to 0.2. Now PIVOTING to a structurally-different knob family (rule 9): the structural/depth knob. The combine grid's best-R-p@3 config was eta0.1_d3_cs0.5 (p3=0.785) and eta0.2_d3_g0.5 had strong p10=0.594, suggesting depth=3 can sharpen the mid-rank tail. Testing max_depth 4->3 at eta=0.2 to see if shallower trees lift p3/p5/p10 while holding the strong p1. Gap is still negative (no overfit) so this is a capacity/shape probe, not regularization. One structural change + the eta revert (the revert isn't a new exploration axis, just returning to the established sweet spot).",
    "iter_2 (depth=3) result: eval R-p@1=0.78, p3=0.697, p5=0.649, p10=0.573 \u2014 depth=3 only marginally beats iter_0 (depth=4) at p10 (0.573 vs 0.572) and LOSES on p1/p3/p5 (0.895/0.743/0.690 vs 0.780/0.697/0.649). So depth=4 (iter_0) is the structural sweet spot. I've now mapped TWO knob families and iter_0 dominates: learning-rate (eta 0.1 hurt R-p, 0.2 best) and structural depth (3 vs 4, 4 best). Reverting depth to 4. Third probe = column-subsampling family (rule 9 wants >=2 structurally-different families before should_stop; this is a 3rd): the scout/combine showed this cell TOLERATES column subsampling without losing top-1 (cs=0.5 was a top performer, cs=0.3 hurt). Testing colsample_bytree 0.5->0.4 to see if slightly stronger feature subsampling sharpens the top-1 tail further OR confirms 0.5 is the sweet spot. Gap is negative (no overfit) so this is a tail-shape probe, not regularization. If cs=0.4 doesn't beat iter_0's R-p@1=0.895, the loop has converged on iter_0 across 3 knob families and I'll should_stop."
